Shorten the startup-entry trace so it survives a phone panel.

kernel_startup_initialize_upto() with startup_debug=0x1 prints

  kernel_startup_initialize_upto[16, rank 2147483647]: 0xfffffe0007ab7cf4(0)

which is 73 characters and wraps onto two console lines.  On a 1080x2340
panel the wrapped tail lands on the last console line, and on raphael the
display's rounded corner clips exactly that line -- the one line that says
where a wedged boot stopped.  Triangulating it from photographs cost an
afternoon.

Print "K<subsystem><low 16 bits of func>" instead: seven characters, never
wraps, never reaches the corner, and the low 16 bits still identify the
entry against the kernel's symbol table.  Nothing else changes; the phase
names kernel_startup_log() prints ("kernel_bootstrap: bsd_init" and friends)
are short enough already.

--- a/osfmk/kern/startup.c
+++ b/osfmk/kern/startup.c
@@ -401,8 +401,10 @@
 	while (cur < startup_entries_end && cur->subsystem <= upto) {
 		if ((startup_debug & STARTUP_DEBUG_VERBOSE) &&
 		    startup_phase >= STARTUP_SUB_KPRINTF) {
-			kprintf("%s[%d, rank %d]: %p(%p)\n", __func__,
-			    cur->subsystem, cur->rank, cur->func, cur->arg);
+			/* SWEETTRACE: seven chars, so the last line of a
+			 * wedged boot stays clear of the rounded corner. */
+			kprintf("K%02x%04x\n", (unsigned)cur->subsystem,
+			    (unsigned)((uintptr_t)cur->func & 0xffffu));
 		}
 		startup_phase = cur->subsystem - 1;
 		kernel_startup_log(cur->subsystem);
